Yay, Holy War! I think there was a recent thread on this topic, which I 
can't find.

If you can devise another means of ensuring a level playing field with 
client-server competitive Nethack, I'd be very interested to hear it. A 
client-server architecture has IMHO too many advantages to give up on.

Of course, it could be possible to mod a telnet client to do this now, 
but it would be hard. Modding a Python Nethack client would be much 
easier.

> > [ example: picking up a cockatrice corpse whilst ungloved ]

The only examples I can come up with are rubber chicken and walking into 
known uncovered traps, including water. And attacking Eyes, which could 
be avoided by m-moving. Oh, wait, it could overcome the obviously 
intentional Stormbringer peacefuls effect. Damn. It'd make sense to have 
the peaceful-not-attacking code client side, making it very easy to mod 
out the Stormbringer override.

> > a) Unspoiled newbie. Painful learning experience. Not fun.
> 
> If our hypothetical player, newbie or otherwise, finds painful
> learning experiences non-fun, then nethack is not the game for
> them. If a game provides no painful learning experiences, then the
> game in question is not nethack, and is thus un-fun for (at least) an
> important subset of nethack players.

That particular painful lesson could also be painlessly learnt by 
reading spoilers. Use of spoilers is not generally considered unsporting 
in remote play. I *think* any informational help a client could provide 
could also easily be learnt from spoilers. Anything dealing with 
strategy rather than simple fact would be too complex for a client to 
help with, at least without difficulty and without excessively annoying 
the player.

> > b) Pressed wrong key. D'oh! The UI _should_ be helping to avoiding 
> > these.
> 
> See above. The UI should make it easy for me to command the game. It
> should make no value judgments as to which actions are wise or unwise.

IMO a UI should also try to ensure the commands you give are the 
commands you intended. For example, a "close window" button should not 
be next to a "maximise window" button. Even if it usually is.

Imagine a GUI in which a food item could be dragged to a dinner plate. 
The dragged item's outline is displayed during dragging whilst the text 
description appears in an info bar. This would make it just about 
impossible to accidentally eat a cockatrice corpse. Do you think the 
difference between "just about" and "absolutely" is sufficient to allow 
this GUI, or would you also object to this GUI?

